A1. Not fair (Not a brunette, either). ~ FOUL
A5. This will tell on a fat lady. ~ SCALE
A10. You can depend on this kind of man for a quick getaway. ~ FAST
A14. The more of this you have, the more horse-like you are. ~ SENSE
A15. The reason why missionaries leave home. ~ PAGAN
A16. What Frenchmen use to measure beer with. ~ LITER
A18. If you're this don't tell us you're a 100% American. ~ ALIEN
A19. To marry in haste. ~ ELOPE
A20. A very Rich girl. ~ IRENE
A21. Moanin' low in the green pastures. ~ MOO
A22. Those along the Bowery. ~ THEM
A24. That knock at the chamber-door. ~ RAP
A26. This's how poets get even. ~ EEN
A27. This is bored to death in France. ~ ENNUIED
A29. A good advertiser. ~ SIGN
A31. A note fit for ladies only. ~ ALTO
A32. What most war spies had to be. ~ SLY
A33. These like to hold hands at bridge parties. ~ PLAYERS
A35. Dollar makers. ~ DIMES
A38. A pretty tight customer. ~ CLAM
A39. A lover. ~ MUSHER
A43. The Wickersham Report made this kind of reading. ~ ARID
A44. A lodging for the night (slang). ~ FLOP
A45. This's been in the pen for years. ~ HOG
A46. You may yet get a few hops out of this. ~ ALE
A47. You'll find this in every best cellar. ~ TON
A48. There are twelve speakeasies at least for every one of these—ask any policeman. ~ BEAT
A49. This stands for Prohibition (plural). ~ BANS
A50. This's pretty well worn out. ~ RAG
A51. This will finish an exciting romance. ~ END
A52. A seafaring eagle. ~ ERN
A53. This'll make you yawn. ~ BORE
A54. So far, this's the second Greek character we've met. ~ BETA
A55. Efficiency expert's racket. ~ SYSTEM
A57. Baby talk. ~ LISP
A58. This hardly ever takes a drink. (It couldn't be a dry officer, could it?) ~ CAMEL
A59. Beware of these. ~ ENEMIES
A61. Short for collocate. ~ PUT
A62. Something you've got to take along when you marry. ~ MATE
A65. "Never"—thus sadly said the poet. ~ NEER
A66. When this changed things began to drop and many joints had to go under cover. ~ FASHION
A70. Before long. ~ ERE
A71. This is 'It', possessively speaking. ~ ITS
A72. An old Scotch measure. ~ PINT
A73. This's worse than a saxophone solo. ~ DUO
A74. Encore. ~ AGAIN
A76. A key for drunkards. ~ MINOR
A79. Here's where bulls and bears ought to fight it out. ~ ARENA
A81. Easy pickings for burglars. ~ LOCKS
A82. These are awfully wicked. ~ EVILS
A83. Well-fed boarder's asset. ~ REACH
A84. Many a Quaker'll swear this is you. ~ THEE
A85. This man is just chuck full of mechanical devices. ~ ROBOT
A86. What an Englishman gets when he's out of a job. ~ DOLE

D1. A first-class pen man. ~ FELON
D2. After the honeymoon is over this starts brides a-crying. ~ ONION
D3. What employers do. ~ USE
D4. These are best in the soup. ~ LENTILS
D5. What you must have if you want to live a fast life ~ SPEED
D6. "All Quiet—" ~ CALM
D7. If you ever get lumbago this will end it. ~ AGO
D8. This is pretty soft for the flappers. ~ LAP
